{"hash": "0f1691cb7cb18000", "text": "Five years ago, in 2021, no mainstream coding agent ran commands for you. The person at the keyboard typed every C D, and only that person moved the dot. Claude Code opened as a limited research preview in the terminal in February 2025. Codex C L I followed in April 2025. Now a coding agent runs commands from a working directory it inherited, and it types the C D itself. Claude Code's shell keeps its folder from one command to the next. Users have reported that a C D out of the project, or out of an added directory, gets pulled back to the project folder, and the result says so. Subagents don't carry a C D over. The sandbox runs on macOS, Linux, and Windows Subsystem for Linux version two, per the docs read this month. Native Windows runs commands without that sandbox, so on Windows, the folder rule is the one that matters. Some people have reported the folder resetting between every command. That is a reported issue, not a rule. A worktree is a second folder for the same project, on its own branch, sharing one history. The detail is 08.11. Agents can resolve paths for you when they know the folder. You still need to know which folder they're standing in, and whether it's the project you think. Where this goes next is a guess. Agents will probably do more of the typing, and the question will still be the same.", "alignment": {"characters": ["F", "i", "v", "e", " ", "y", "e", "a", "r", "s", " ", "a", "g", "o", ",", " ", "i", "n", " ", "2", "0", "2", "1", ",", " ", "n", "o", " ", "m", "a", "i", "n", "s", "t", "r", "e", "a", "m", " ", "c", "o", "d", "i", "n", "g", " ", "a", "g", "e", "n", "t", " ", "r", "a", "n", " ", "c", "o", "m", "m", "a", "n", "d", "s", " ", "f", "o", "r", " ", "y", "o", "u", ".", " ", "T", "h", "e", " ", "p", "e", "r", "s", "o", "n", " ", "a", "t", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "k", "e", "y", "b", "o", "a", "r", "d", " ", "t", "y", "p", "e", "d", " ", "e", "v", "e", "r", "y", " ", "C", " ", "D", ",", " ", "a", "n",